# linux-pam 1.6.1
#
# pam_systemd is how a login becomes a logind session; gdm needs the stack.
name: linux-pam
version:
- '1'
- '6'
- '1'
dependencies: []
steps:

- stage: Prepare
  name: fetch
  dl_urls:
    @URL_LINUX_PAM@: '@SHA256_LINUX_PAM@'
  run:
  - mkdir -p /build/src/linux-pam /build/b/linux-pam
  # --no-same-owner: pm's jail is a user namespace mapping only uid 0, so a
  # tarball recording any other uid fails tar at the very end.
  - tar -xf @DL_LINUX_PAM@ --no-same-owner -C /build/src/linux-pam --strip-components=1

# ./configure has no -C and there is no `cd` in a build step (C1), so the one
# permitted wrapper puts it in the build directory. configure takes VAR=value
# as positional arguments, which is how the toolchain and the sysroot are
# reached without `env`.
#
# CFLAGS and LDFLAGS are clang @response-files, not flag lists: pm splits a
# command on whitespace, so a multi-word CFLAGS would arrive as a dozen
# separate arguments and configure would reject the first it did not know.
# The response file is one word, and clang expands it. Its contents come from
# manifest/toolchain.yaml -- LTO and CFI included.
#
# The flags are this package's own response files, not the layer's shared
# pair, and naming them is the entire mechanism: manifest/toolchain.yaml
# gives linux-pam `drops: [visibility]`, tools/configure writes
# cflags-linux-pam.rsp for it either way, and a recipe left spelling
# @CFLAGS_RSP@ gets the shared flags with the exemption written to disk and
# read by nothing. Without it libpam installs empty and systemd's own
# PAM support is the first thing to say so.
#
# --disable-doc is load-bearing beyond the documentation, and the reason is
# not local to this recipe. linux-pam is the third spelling in this tree of
# the build-machine-compiler problem that libgpg-error and nettle carry:
# configure.ac:85-94 reaches for one when cross-compiling, and it reaches with
# AC_CHECK_PROGS(CC_FOR_BUILD, gcc cc). The build container has neither, and
# AC_CHECK_PROGS with no fallback leaves the variable EMPTY and does not fail
# -- configure prints "checking for CC_FOR_BUILD... " with nothing after it
# and carries on. The one place that spends it is doc/specs/Makefile.am:14,
# `CC = @CC_FOR_BUILD@`, which would make CC itself empty for that directory.
#
# --disable-doc clears HAVE_DOC (configure.ac:222-225), which drops doc from
# SUBDIRS (Makefile.am:10-12), so doc/specs is never entered and the empty
# variable is never spent. Dropping the flag to get man pages would therefore
# turn on a failure that has nothing to do with documentation; add
# CC_FOR_BUILD=@CC@ at the same time, the way the other two recipes do.
#
# pam_cv_ld_no_undefined=no is the third spelling of the --no-undefined
# problem, after tpm2-tss's --disable-defaultflags and kmod's OUR_LDFLAGS=,
# and it is answered the way this tree answers every configure-time probe that
# asks for a flag cross-DSO CFI cannot survive: the cache variable, set on the
# configure line, which is a documented autoconf interface and not a patch.
#
# configure.ac:76 runs PAM_LD_NO_UNDEFINED (m4/ld-no-undefined.m4), which links
# a trivial executable with -Wl,--no-undefined and appends the flag to LDFLAGS
# when the link succeeds. It does succeed here, for exactly the reason
# tpm2-tss's probe does: an executable link is where clang puts
# libclang_rt.cfi_diag, so the probe's own link resolves the two symbols and
# the flag lands in LDFLAGS for every link after it. libpam.la is the first
# one that is a SHARED object, where clang links no sanitizer runtime at all,
# and it stopped there:
#
#     mold: error: undefined symbol: __ubsan_handle_cfi_check_fail
#     >>> referenced by pam_account.c
#     mold: error: undefined symbol: __cfi_slowpath_diag
#     >>> referenced by pam_data.c
#     clang: error: linker command failed with exit code 1
#     make[2]: *** [Makefile:573: libpam.la] Error 1
#
# The flag comes from this probe alone. It is not in the response file --
# out/recipes/losos-10-base/ldflags-linux-pam.rsp carries --as-needed and
# every CFI flag but no --no-undefined -- so what appears on the link line is
# LDFLAGS as configure accumulated it, spelled out ahead of the response file:
#
#     @.../ldflags-linux-pam.rsp -Wl,--as-needed -Wl,--no-undefined -Wl,-O1
#
# Setting the cache variable suppresses the probe, and the tail disappears with
# it. That is why the fix is here and not in the response file: there is no
# --no-undefined in the response file to remove.
#
# Nor could there be. LDFLAGS is a single word -- a clang @response-file@,
# because pm splits a command on whitespace (C1) -- so configure's own probes
# read the response file too, and any file this recipe named would be the one
# the probes use. The probe is the only thing that adds the flag, so the probe
# is the only place to stop it.
#
# Nothing else in the PAM_LD_* set needs this: --as-needed is harmless and
# -O1 is a linker optimisation. Only --no-undefined asks the linker to refuse
# the two symbols cross-DSO CFI deliberately leaves undefined, and the
# exemption is linux-pam's `drops: [visibility]` -- which keeps every CFI
# scheme, and so keeps those two calls, while only relaxing the hidden
# visibility CFI wants as a precondition.
#
# The check-exports test below is what keeps this honest: libpam.map and
# libpam_internal would both link to an empty libpam.so if the exemption were
# ever narrowed, and the nm check on pam_start is the thing that would say so.
#
# --disable-logind because the logind probe answers yes from the wrong tree.
# configure.ac:564 runs, under the default WITH_LOGIND=check,
#
#     PKG_CHECK_MODULES([SYSTEMD], [libsystemd >= 254],
#                       [LOGIND_CFLAGS="-DUSE_LOGIND=1 $SYSTEMD_CFLAGS"], [:])
#
# and it succeeds -- libsystemd.pc is found -- while the header it names is
# not in the sysroot:
#
#     checking for libsystemd >= 254... yes
#     ...
#     pam_issue.c:32:10: fatal error: 'systemd/sd-login.h' file not found
#
# systemd is four layers above this one (losos-20-systemd) and this layer
# cannot build its headers; only pam_issue.c and pam_timestamp.c read
# USE_LOGIND at all. PKG_CONFIG_PATH names the sysroot's .pc directories but
# cannot subtract the host's: pkg-config's own compiled-in default path,
# /usr/lib/pkgconfig among it, is still searched, and the host has libsystemd
# where the sysroot does not. Without the flag the probe reads the host and
# defines USE_LOGIND against nothing; with it the two modules fall back to
# their utmp path, which is what the sysroot actually carries (utmp.h is
# present: "checking for utmp.h... yes").
#
# lvm2, one layer over, turns systemd off for the same reason and says so in
# the same words -- "--disable-systemd-journal and --disable-sd-notify because
# systemd is four layers above". When pam_systemd.so is actually needed, the
# fix is a real libsystemd in this layer, not this flag.
- stage: Build
  name: configure
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - /bin/sh @RECIPE@/in-dir.sh /build/b/linux-pam /build/src/linux-pam/configure --host=@ARCH_TRIPLE@ --prefix=/usr --libdir=/usr/lib --sysconfdir=/etc --localstatedir=/var CC=@CC@ AR=@AR@ NM=@NM@ RANLIB=@RANLIB@ CFLAGS=@CFLAGS_RSP_LINUX_PAM@ LDFLAGS=@LDFLAGS_RSP_LINUX_PAM@ PKG_CONFIG_PATH=/build/sysroot/usr/lib/pkgconfig:/build/sysroot/usr/share/pkgconfig pam_cv_ld_no_undefined=no --disable-static --disable-doc --disable-logind

- stage: Build
  name: compile
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - make -C /build/b/linux-pam -j @JOBS@

# DESTDIR as a make argument, not the environment variable: pm already sets
# DESTDIR=/dest, and the second install has to override it.
- stage: Build
  name: install
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - make -C /build/b/linux-pam install DESTDIR=/dest
  - make -C /build/b/linux-pam install DESTDIR=/build/sysroot
  - /bin/sh @RECIPE@/stage-sysroot.sh /build/sysroot

- stage: Test
  name: staged
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - test -e /dest/usr/lib/libpam.so
  - /bin/sh @RECIPE@/check-exports.sh @NM@ /dest/usr/lib/libpam.so pam_start pam_authenticate pam_open_session pam_end
